insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 1, 'A mapping algorithm accepts control points, not wells. What does each Ekene well reduce to before anything can be gridded?', '["An x, a y and a z taken from the pick","A measured depth, an inclination and an azimuth from the deviation survey, one row per station","Shale volume, porosity and saturation at the level of the top","An x, a y, and the thickness between the top and the base"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A control point is three numbers: horizontal position from the well''s map location and a depth from the pick that correlation produced. Everything else known about the well is set aside for the purposes of this map, which is why a single bad pick is so damaging.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 2, 'Which one of these questions can be answered from the table of six Ekene picks alone, with no map at all?', '["Where the shallowest point of the TOP_SAND surface lies","Which drilled well has the shallowest TOP_SAND pick","How large the closure is in map area","What gross rock volume sits above an assumed contact"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A table tells you which well is shallowest; it cannot tell you where the surface is shallowest, because that is often at a location no well reached. Closure is a shape and volume is an integral, so both need a continuous surface.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 3, 'The Ekene TOP_SAND picks run from 1541 m at Ekene-3 to 1590 m at Ekene-4. How much relief does the control set show?', '["5 m","25 m","1541 m","49 m"]'::jsonb, 3, '1590 minus 1541 is 49 m of relief across the six wells, which the lesson reads as real structure rather than noise. The 25 m is the gap between Ekene-4 and the next deepest well, Ekene-2 at 1565 m, the 5 m is the gap between the two shallowest picks, and 1541 m is the shallowest pick itself rather than a difference between picks.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 4, 'Reading the Ekene control set before gridding, in which direction does the surface fall away most steeply and into which well?', '["To the southwest, into Ekene-1 at 1548 m","To the west, into Ekene-5 at 1552 m","To the northeast, into Ekene-4 at 1590 m","To the north, into Ekene-3 at 1541 m"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-4 sits in the far northeast at 1590 m, 25 m deeper than the next deepest well and 49 m deeper than Ekene-3, so the surface drops hard toward the northeast. Ekene-3 in the north is the shallowest pick, so the surface rises that way rather than falling.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 5, 'A seventh Ekene well is drilled, but the interpreter honestly cannot identify TOP_SAND in it. What should happen to the control set?', '["The well enters the control set carrying the average of the six other picks, so the coverage is not lost","The well enters carrying a z of zero, which the algorithm reads as missing","The well is absent from the control set","The map is rejected until a pick is forced in that well"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well with no pick is one fewer control point, so the map over that part of the field is that much less supported, exactly as a missing top left a correlation line short instead of being bridged. Filling the gap with an average or a zero invents a control point that no measurement supports, and no downstream user can see that it was invented.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 6, 'A deviated well lands its TOP_SAND pick several hundred metres laterally from its wellhead. Depths have been properly converted to true vertical depth subsea, but the pick is posted at the wellhead coordinates. Which part of the control point is wrong?', '["The z, because measured depth along a deviated hole is longer than the vertical distance","The x and the y, while the z is correct","All three numbers","None of them, because the pick still belongs to that well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Posting at the wellhead puts a correct depth at a place the bit never reached, so the map bends around a point in the wrong location. The error is silent because the control point still looks perfectly legal.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 7, 'Two wells are drilled from rigs with different drill floor elevations and their picks are never referenced to a common sea level zero. What appears on the map?', '["A correct structure, since both wells picked the same formation","A shift in the x and y of both wells","Nothing unusual, because the datum cancels during gridding","A false structure, since the wrong z sits on correct coordinates"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Subtracting the datum elevation is what puts every well on the same vertical reference. Skip it and picks appear to differ in depth where the rock does not, which is structure created entirely by bookkeeping.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 8, 'Prospect P-1 sits at (1600, 1600) with no well on it. Why does the course call it a fair test of the map rather than an unfair one?', '["Its nearest well, Ekene-6, is about 361 m away","It sits exactly halfway between the shallowest and the deepest well","It lies outside the well pattern, so it tests the extrapolation limit","It already has a pick that the mapped value can be checked against"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene-6 lies about 361 m from P-1, comfortably inside the well spacing of the field, so a prediction there rests on genuine nearby control and tests the map rather than punishing it. Nothing has been drilled at P-1, so there is no pick to compare the mapped value against.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 9, 'Which two Ekene wells were not part of the correlation course, and why do they matter to the map?', '["Ekene-1 and Ekene-2, because they are the two southernmost wells and anchor that edge of the grid frame","Ekene-3 and Ekene-4, because they bracket the depth range","Ekene-2 and Ekene-5, because they sit on the section line","Ekene-5 and Ekene-6, because they sit off the section line that the correlation course used"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A map needs control spread out rather than lined up. A correlation section is one dimensional in map view and has no way to show a well off to the side, so Ekene-5 and Ekene-6 were of no use there. Four wells strung along a line say almost nothing about what the surface does perpendicular to that line.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 10, 'In what order does the course make the four mapping decisions?', '["Interpolation, grid, presentation, extrapolation","Grid, extrapolation, interpolation, presentation","Grid, interpolation, extrapolation, presentation","Presentation, grid, interpolation, extrapolation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The grid frame comes first because everything else is written onto it, then interpolation fills between the control points, then the extrapolation limit decides how far the map may reach, and presentation is last. The decisions are not independent; the first one constrains what the third can do.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 11, 'The capstone grid frame contains 500 nodes and only 201 of them carry a value. Which of the four decisions is responsible for the 299 that do not?', '["The choice of a 100 m cell size, which sets how many nodes fall outside the mapped area","The extrapolation limit, which masks nodes far from any control point","The thin-plate interpolation, which declines to fill space between wells","The 10 m contour interval, which discards values between levels"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Masking is the work of decision three, the extrapolation limit, which blanks any node further than 800 m from the nearest control point, although decision one set how many nodes there were to mask in the first place. Interpolation is what fills space between control points, so it is not what leaves nodes blank.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 12, 'What interpolation method does this course use, and what does it do at a control point?', '["An average of the six picks, applied at every node","A nearest neighbour rule, which copies the closest pick to each node","A planar fit through the three shallowest wells","A thin-plate spline fitted through all six control points"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The spline returns each well''s own pick at that well''s location. It behaves like a thin sheet of metal bent to pass through every control point while minimising how hard it has to bend overall, so it honours the data exactly and gives a smooth surface between wells rather than a faceted one.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 13, 'The mapped crest at the capstone setting is 1539.72 m, while the shallowest pick in the fixture is Ekene-3 at 1541 m. What does that tell you?', '["The map puts the highest point of the structure where no well was drilled","The Ekene-3 pick is about 1.3 m too deep and should be corrected to match the mapped crest","The grid has failed to honour its control points","The contour interval was set too coarse for this surface"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A crest shallower than any pick is the map making a claim about ground no well reached. It is neither an error nor a guarantee, and deciding which it is here is the work of the rest of the course.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 14, 'This course caps extrapolation at 800 m. What happens to a grid node that sits 900 m from the nearest control point?', '["It takes the value of the nearest control point","It takes the mean of the mapped surface","It is masked, left blank and drawn as nothing, carrying no value at all","It is given a value flagged as low confidence and contoured normally"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Beyond the limit the map declines to guess, so the node is not given a value at all. Drawing it as a confident colour, or as the nearest pick, would present inference with the same weight as measurement.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-maps-and-control-points', 15, 'How does the course summarise the difference between what correlation tells you and what mapping tells you?', '["Correlation matches surfaces between wells, while mapping gives a value everywhere","Correlation works in map view while mapping works along a section line drawn between wells","Correlation is quantitative while mapping is qualitative","Correlation describes the field as a whole while mapping describes what happens in one well at a time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mapping is the first step whose output covers ground no well has reached, which is what makes it powerful and what makes it dangerous. Petrophysics works down one well, correlation works laterally between wells, and mapping produces a value everywhere in a defined area, including between and beyond them.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 1, 'A summary panel reports a count of nodes. What is it counting?', '["The wells that contributed a pick to the map","The cells enclosed by the grid, each with an area on the ground","The contour lines drawn on the finished map","Lattice positions, each of which is a location holding at most one value"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A node is one lattice position. It is not a cell, not a well and not a pick, and confusing a node count with a count of wells or cells is the fastest way to overstate what a map established.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 2, 'Why can a scattered set of six control points not be contoured directly?', '["Six points is too few for any contouring algorithm to run","The points carry no depth values until they have been gridded","Loose points have no defined neighbours for a contour to follow","Contours can only be drawn on data sampled at regular time intervals"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A contour is traced by asking on each cell edge where the chosen depth falls between the two ends, which requires knowing which node is next to which. A lattice answers that by construction; a point cloud has no path for a contour to follow.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 3, 'Why does subtracting a base surface from a top surface to get thickness require both surfaces to sit on the same frame?', '["Because thickness is a node-by-node subtraction","Because the two surfaces must end up with the same live node count","Because the engine can only hold one grid in memory at a time","Because the two maps must share a contour interval before anything can be subtracted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Differencing is a node-by-node subtraction, so array position 137 has to mean the same place on the ground in both grids. Two clouds of points measured at different wells cannot be subtracted at all.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 4, 'You ask for a depth at a proposed well location where nothing has been drilled. How does a grid make that answerable when a point set does not?', '["The location falls inside one cell whose four corner nodes bracket it","The grid stores a value at every possible position on the ground","The grid returns the pick from the nearest well instead","The grid averages all its live nodes and returns that mean"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sampling needs coverage, and a lattice provides it: any location inside the frame sits within a cell whose four corner nodes bracket it. On a point set the honest answer is that there is no value at that location.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 5, 'Of the four operations a grid supports, integration is the one that needs something a point set can never provide. What is it?', '["A shared origin between two surfaces, so they line up node for node","A regularly sampled time axis","Defined neighbours, so a line can step from one measurement to the next","Area, since each cell carries a constant footprint"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gross rock volume is a sum over the map area, and each cell brings a known and constant footprint to that sum. Points have no area, so there is nothing to integrate over.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 6, 'What does the frame, also called the spec, consist of?', '["An origin, a count of nodes across, a count of nodes up, and the cell size","The depth value stored at every node of the map","The six control points together with the interpolation method","The live node count and the contour interval"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The frame is geometry with no depth values in it yet; give the engine those four things and the position of every node follows. It says nothing about which nodes end up carrying a value.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 7, 'The engine pads the box around the control points by two cells on every side. What follows from the pad being measured in cells rather than in metres?', '["The pad is always 200 m wide, whatever cell size you choose","The pad grows with the cell size","The pad shrinks as the cell size grows, so a coarse grid hugs the wells more tightly","The pad is applied only on the eastern and northern sides of the control box"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two cells of pad is two cells whatever those cells measure, so the pad is 200 m each side at a 100 m cell and 400 m each side at a 200 m cell, and the frame changes shape in metres when you change the cell size. Treating the pad as a fixed 200 m is the error that makes coarse frames come out too small.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 8, 'The Ekene control spans x from 600 to 2600 and y from 1000 to 2500. At a 100 m cell size, where does the frame origin sit?', '["(600, 1000), the corner of the control box itself","(500, 900)","(400, 800)","(800, 400)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two cells of pad at 100 m is 200 m, taken off the western and southern edges: 600 minus 200 is 400, and 1000 minus 200 is 800. The (500, 900) is the origin you get at a 50 m cell, where the pad is only 100 m.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 9, 'The 100 m frame runs from x = 400 to x = 2800. How many nodes across, and why?', '["24, because 2400 divided by 100 is 24 intervals across the frame","26, because the origin and the far edge each add a node to the 24","23, because the two pad cells at each end are not counted as nodes","25, because 24 intervals need 25 nodes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is the fence-post rule: 24 is the number of gaps and 25 is the number of nodes, as a 24 panel fence needs 25 posts. Answering 24 shifts every number downstream and the map still draws, so the error does not announce itself.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 10, 'The capstone frame is 25 nodes across and 20 up, so it holds 500 nodes. What does the number 500 tell you?', '["How many depth values the finished map actually carries","How finely you chose to sample the area","How well six wells support the field","The area of the map in hectares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Total nodes is a decision about coverage and resolution, and it says nothing about which nodes carry a value. The live count is the separate number that says how much of that area the control could support, and at this setting it is 201 rather than 500.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 11, 'At the capstone setting 201 of the 500 nodes are live. Where does that split come from?', '["From the frame, which does not give values to the padded rows","From the extrapolation rule, not from anything in the frame","From the contour interval, which drops nodes that fall between levels","From the six control points, which cap the live count at 201"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The frame fixes where the 500 nodes sit and says nothing about which of them carry a depth. Liveness comes from the rule that masks nodes too far from control, which is a decision about honesty rather than about geometry.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 12, 'The same six wells gridded at 50 m, 100 m and 200 m give crests of 1539.691 m, 1539.718 m and 1539.718 m, with live node counts of 794, 201 and 50. What do those two columns show?', '["The geology changes with the cell size, which is why both columns move","The crest is unreliable while the live count is a robust measure of the field","The crest barely moves because the surface is the same, while the live count is a grid property","Both columns are properties of the geology, so neither depends on the setting"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Across a factor of four in cell size the crest shifts by 0.027 m on a surface with about 50 m of relief, because the same six points and the same interpolation rule define it, and only the sampling differs. Node counts look like results but are properties of the grid, so quoting 794 live nodes as evidence of a well understood field is the classic misreading.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 13, 'At a 200 m cell size the Ekene frame comes out 13 nodes up. How?', '["7.5 rounds up to 8 intervals, plus 1 node and 4 for the pad","1500 divided by 200 is 7.5, rounded down to 7, plus 6 for the pad","2500 divided by 200 is 12.5, rounded up to 13","1500 divided by 200 is 7.5, plus 5.5 for the pad"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The control span in y is 1500 m, which 200 does not divide evenly, so the engine rounds the interval count up to 8 to make sure the frame still covers the control. The fence-post one and the four pad cells then give 13.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 14, 'The 50 m and 100 m maps are contoured at a 10 m interval, but the 200 m map drops to 5 m. Why?', '["Coarse grids are given more lines to disguise their blockiness","The extrapolation limit is applied twice at coarse cell sizes","The interpolation method changes below a 100 m cell","The range of mapped values is narrower at a 200 m cell"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The interval is chosen from the range of values present on the grid. No node lands on Ekene-4 at a 200 m cell, so the deepest pick of 1590 m never appears on the map, the range is narrower, and aiming for about ten contours across a narrower range gives a finer step. The coarser and less supported map ends up drawn with twice as many contour lines and looks more detailed.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-grid', 15, 'At the capstone frame five of the six wells land exactly on nodes. Why does Ekene-2 at (2200, 1150) not?', '["Its x coordinate is not a multiple of the cell size","It falls outside the padded frame","Its y sits three and a half cells above the origin","It was dropped from the control set as an outlier before gridding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A well lands on a node only when its offset from the origin is a whole number of cells in both directions, and its y sits 350 m above the origin at 800, which is 3.5 cells, so the nearest nodes are 50 m to either side. A node 50 m from a well is still not the well; it holds a value the algorithm wrote.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 1, 'Interpolation produces a value at a location where nothing was measured. What does the method itself supply to that value?', '["New information about the rock at that node, extracted from the six picks","An assumption about how surfaces behave, applied consistently everywhere","A physical calculation of depth from the measured picks","A measurement carried across from the nearest well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing in six picks contains information about the rock 400 m away, so interpolation is not a physical calculation. What it adds is a stated assumption about surface behaviour applied the same way at every node, which is what makes the map describable, defensible and repeatable.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 2, 'Under nearest neighbour, what value does a node 500 m from Ekene-6 receive, assuming no other well is closer?', '["A value between 1546 m and the next nearest pick, weighted by distance","No value, because 500 m is too far for a single pick to govern","1546 m, the same value a node 50 m from Ekene-6 would receive","1550.27 m, the mean of the mapped surface"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nearest neighbour assigns the closest control point''s value with no taper at all, so every node in a well''s tile carries that well''s pick. The result is flat tiles with cliffs at the boundaries, and every number on the map is a real pick that may be in the wrong place.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 3, 'What is the characteristic artefact of inverse distance weighting on a control set like Ekene?', '["A small dome or pit forming tight around each of the six control points","Straight contour segments with kinks along triangle edges","Flat tiles separated by step boundaries","A crest that rises above the shallowest pick"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A well''s weight runs away as you approach it, so the surface forms a tight circular dome or pit at each control point and the map picks up six rings that reflect the algorithm. Kinked contours belong to triangulation, flat tiles to nearest neighbour, and overshoot to the spline.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 4, 'Two mappers use the same six Ekene picks with different interpolation methods and get different maps. Where do the two maps differ, and what settles which one is right?', '["They differ at the wells, and the pick list settles it","They differ everywhere, and the smoother of the two is the better map","They differ only at the frame corners, and the grid settings settle it","They differ between the wells, and no measurement exists there to settle it"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every one of the four families returns the measured value at the measured location, so the maps agree completely about the data. They disagree about the space between the data, which is most of the map and is exactly where there is no observation to referee the argument.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 5, 'What surface does a thin-plate spline choose?', '["The surface that stays closest to the average of the six picks","The surface that passes exactly through every control point while minimising total bending","The surface that passes through every control point and flattens to horizontal away from them","The flattest plane that misses the six picks by the smallest total amount"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The mental picture is a thin sheet forced onto six pins and otherwise left to relax into the shape that stores the least bending energy. The last option describes a least squares fit, which misses every well and is therefore not an interpolator at all.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 6, 'The shallowest pick at Ekene is 1541 m and the shallowest node on the gridded map is 1539.7181 m. What has happened?', '["The solver failed to honour Ekene-3, so the fit should be rejected","Rounding in the float arithmetic accumulated across the grid","The sheet bows past its own pins in between them","A seventh control point was created by the decimation step"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A flexible sheet bent through fixed pins rises above them in between, which is what a smooth spline does, so a spline is not trapped inside the range of the data the way an averaging method is. The 1.28 m overshoot is the method behaving as designed, and it is a prediction rather than a measurement.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 7, 'Ekene-4''s pick is corrected from 1590 m to 1585 m and the map is regridded. Which nodes take a new value?', '["All live nodes, because every control point enters the sum for every node","Only nodes within one cell of Ekene-4, since the correction is local","Only nodes in the north east quadrant, where Ekene-4 has influence","No nodes, because the deepest mapped value is clipped to the pick"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fit is global: the whole linear system is resolved and every node picks up a contribution from every well, including nodes on the far side of the field. Most move by a tiny amount and none are untouched, which is why a bad pick is contagious with a spline.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 8, 'How many of the six Ekene control points does the engine use for the fit?', '["Three, which is the minimum the solver requires","Five, because one point is dropped as a near duplicate","Six for the plane terms and three for the radial terms","All six, because six is under the decimation ceiling"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Decimation exists to keep the dense solve affordable and fires only beyond a default ceiling of 700 points. Six is far under that ceiling, so all six are used and the dropped count is zero.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 9, 'Why does the fitted spline return 1590 m when it is evaluated at Ekene-4''s own coordinates?', '["Because the engine writes the picks back over the grid after fitting","Because the linear system contains a row for each pick","Because the node nearest Ekene-4 inherits the pick under a nearest neighbour rule","Because 1590 m is the deepest pick and the solver clamps the surface there"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Exactness is built into the linear system: each control point contributes a row stating that the surface equals that pick, and solving the system is the act of enforcing those equalities. With no smoothing term the solver cannot trade a little misfit at the wells for a little less bending in between, so all the smoothing goes into the space between the pins.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 10, 'The deepest mapped value is 1590 m and the shallowest is 1539.7181 m. What does that pair tell you?', '["Both are measurements, because an exact interpolator reproduces the pick list","Both are products of the algorithm, because both are grid node values","1590 m is a measurement because it equals Ekene-4''s pick, and 1539.7181 m is not","1539.7181 m is a measurement taken between wells and 1590 m is the extrapolated deep limit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The same exact interpolator produced both numbers, and the only way to tell them apart is to compare the mapped extremes against the pick list. At the deep end it returned a measurement, and at the shallow end it returned an invention that no well picked, sitting 1.28 m above the shallowest pick.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 11, 'In which situation would you deliberately choose a method that misses the picks?', '["Thousands of automatically tracked seismic picks","Six hand-checked well tops spread over a few square kilometres","A control set with fewer than three points","Any dataset where the mapped crest overshoots the shallowest pick"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Exactness is a virtue only when the data deserves it. Automatic seismic picks carry scatter that is not structure, and forcing a smooth surface through them makes the algorithm reproduce that scatter, and because a spline is smooth it does so by bending hard, producing dimples and small closures that mean nothing.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 12, 'The Ekene map at a 100 m cell carries 201 values. How many of them are measurements?', '["201, because the spline honours all six picks across the whole map","At most six, and the rest came from the spline alone","Six, and the remaining 195 were each checked against the nearest well","None, because a grid node never coincides with a well location"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At most six nodes coincide with a well, so roughly 195 of the 201 values were produced by the thin-plate spline and by nothing else, about 97 percent of the map. Every one of those values is the output of a defensible method and not one of them was observed.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 13, 'Why is roughly 195 invented values out of 201 not a reason to reject the Ekene map?', '["Because the invented values were validated against the six picks","Because the spline overshoot proves that the surface really is smooth","Because a finer grid would raise the share of measured nodes","Because the alternative is no continuous surface, and no contours"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Six wells over a few square kilometres is an ordinary amount of control at this stage, and a table of six depths cannot be contoured, differenced or turned into a thickness or a volume. The ratio is a permanent label on the product rather than an argument against mapping.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 14, 'A colleague reads a depth off the gridded map and offers it as support for the claim that the surface behaves smoothly between wells. What is wrong with that?', '["Nothing, because the value came from an exact interpolator that honours all six picks","The value should have been read at a well rather than between wells","The map was built from a smoothness assumption","The value is usable only if it is quoted to four decimal places"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An interpolated value is not evidence: the map was built from a smoothness assumption, so the value can only give back the assumption that produced it, which is circular. The extra decimal places make it look authoritative, but the digits come from float arithmetic rather than from knowledge.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-interpolation', 15, 'Which statement about P-1 follows the module''s rule for quoting a mapped value?', '["TOP_SAND at P-1 is about 1543 m from the gridded map, with the nearest control 361 m away at Ekene-6","TOP_SAND at P-1 is 1542.6199 m","TOP_SAND at P-1 is 1546 m, the pick at Ekene-6, which is the nearest well","TOP_SAND at P-1 cannot be quoted at all, because no well has been drilled there to measure it"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Quote map values with their support rather than with the authority of a pick, which takes a sentence instead of a number. Ekene-6 picked 1546 m, and stating the bare figure hides that the estimate rests on control 361 m away. Refusing to give any number leaves a decision that still has to be made with nothing to work from.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 1, 'What distinguishes interpolation from extrapolation on the Ekene map?', '["Interpolation uses all six wells, while extrapolation uses only the nearest one","Interpolation happens inside the grid frame, and extrapolation happens outside it","Interpolation works between control points and extrapolation beyond them","Interpolation is exact at the wells, while extrapolation is exact at the frame corners"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Interpolation is boxed in: a node inside the well pattern cannot drift far, because drifting too shallow would force expensive bending to reach the wells on every side. Extrapolation continues a trend past the control with nothing on the far side to check it, so a node beyond the wells has open ground in front of it and nothing pulls the surface back.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 2, 'Stretch an elastic band around all six Ekene wells and let it snap tight. What have you drawn?', '["The extrapolation limit, at 800 m from the nearest well","The convex hull of the control set, which is the honest outer edge of interpolation","The grid frame, which starts at (400, 800) and runs 25 columns by 20 rows","The 1540 m contour, which closes on itself"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The taut polygon is the convex hull. Inside it you are estimating between wells and outside it you are guessing, which is why the hull is one of the two tests a node must pass before the routine writes a value into it.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 3, 'Why does a thin-plate spline behave badly outside the well control instead of flattening out to something cautious?', '["Its radial term and its plane both grow with distance","It reverts to the mean of the six picks once no well is within range","It reproduces noise in the picks, and that noise is largest at the frame edges","It switches to nearest neighbour behaviour beyond the last control point"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both the radial term and the plane grow with distance, so a trend established inside the data keeps running and runs harder the further out you look. The trend does not politely stop at the last well, and the surface stays perfectly smooth the whole way out, which is precisely the problem. Smoothness is not the same as being right, and a smooth surface looks as authoritative 3 km from control as it does halfway between two wells.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 4, 'Why is a closed contour sitting entirely outside the well control a professional problem rather than an aesthetic one?', '["Because the contour will have been drawn with the wrong contour interval","Because the spline cannot be regridded once contours have been generated","Because it means the well picks themselves were mistracked","Because a closure with an area and a crest becomes a prospect"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Closures are what get drilled, and a closure with an area and a crest becomes a volume and eventually a number in a report that somebody signs. One that exists only outside the control is a classic way for volume to be booked when the rock is not there. It is rarely the result of anyone acting dishonestly, and usually the result of a map that did not distinguish what it knew from what it continued.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 5, 'Before the gridding routine computes anything, what is sitting in the output array?', '["Zeros, which are then overwritten wherever a value can be computed","The null sentinel at every node before anything is written","The mean of the six picks at every node, as a starting guess for the solver","The value of the nearest control point at every node"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every node is filled with the null sentinel first, and a value is written only where the node lies inside the convex hull and has a well within the extrapolation limit. Blank is the default state of the grid, and a value is something a node has to earn.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 6, 'With an 800 m extrapolation limit, what number does the routine compare each node''s squared distance against?', '["800, because the squared distance is square rooted first","400, because the limit is halved for the squared comparison","640,000, because the limit is squared once before the loop over nodes begins","1,600, because the limit is doubled and then squared"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing is square rooted inside the loop. The threshold is 800 times 800, which is 640,000, and every comparison is made in squared units, which is the same test with less arithmetic.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 7, 'A node has one well 640 m away and the next nearest 1100 m away. Under the course limit of 800 m, what happens to it?', '["It is live, and the loop stops at the first qualifying well","It is null, because the average distance to the two wells exceeds 800 m","It is null, because a node needs at least two wells inside the limit","It is live, but its value is weighted down because only one well qualifies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The squared distance 409,600 is less than the threshold of 640,000, so the test passes and the loop breaks at the first control point that qualifies. One well inside the limit is all a node needs, and the mask never alters the value the spline computed.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 8, 'A reviewer suspects the 1.28 m crest overshoot is an artefact of the 800 m extrapolation limit and asks you to tighten the limit to 400 m. What happens?', '["The crest rises further, because fewer nodes now constrain the surface","The crest disappears, because the shallowest node falls outside the tighter limit","The crest and the live count both stay unchanged, because the mask never touches the interior of the map","The live count drops from 201 to 121, while the crest stays at 1539.72 m at the same node"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The spline is fitted before any masking, so the limit changes where values exist and not what they are. The crest node is 300 m from Ekene-3, deep inside the mapped area and nowhere near the mask edge, so the overshoot is an interpolation artefact that no extrapolation setting can remove.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 9, 'What is actually stored at a dead node in the engine''s grid?', '["Zero, which plots as a flat area on the map","The industry export sentinel 1.0e30, meaning nothing was computed","The depth of the nearest well, flagged as unsupported","The mean of the live nodes, so that the reported statistics stay stable"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The null is the sentinel that appears as 1.0000000E+30 in a CPS-3 or ZMAP file. It is not zero and it is not a depth, and treating it as a zero depth is the classic error, because zero is a value while the sentinel is a statement that no value exists.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 10, 'The Ekene map at a 100 m cell reports a mean depth of 1550.27 m. What population is that mean computed over?', '["All 500 frame nodes, with the nulls counted as zero","The six well picks","The 201 live nodes, with nulls never entering the comparison at all","All 500 frame nodes, with the nulls included at their stored value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine increments the live count and updates the running minimum and maximum only in the branch that writes a value, so the reported statistics are live only by construction. Averaging all 500 nodes with the sentinel included would give a mean of roughly 6 times 10 to the 29.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 11, 'The same six picks and the same 800 m limit give 794 live nodes at a 50 m cell, 201 at 100 m and 50 at 200 m. What does that tell you about the live count?', '["It is a property of the grid setup rather than the subsurface","It shows that a finer cell maps a larger area of the field","It shows that the 200 m grid failed to honour three of the six wells","It shows that the surface changes shape as the cell size changes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing about the subsurface differs between those three rows, because only the sampling changed. Saying that the map has 794 live nodes without saying the cell was 50 m says almost nothing, since the same map also has 201 live nodes and 50 live nodes.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 12, 'Which quantity stays nearly the same across the 50 m, 100 m and 200 m versions of the Ekene map?', '["The live node count, which stays close to 200","The live share of the frame, which stays close to 40 percent","The number of frame nodes, which stays close to 500","The mapped area, which lands within about 1 percent of 2.0 million square metres"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each live node stands for one cell of area, so 794 nodes at 2,500 square metres, 201 at 10,000 and 50 at 40,000 all come to about 2 square kilometres. The mapped area is set by the wells and the extrapolation limit, so convert a live count to area if you want a number that survives a change of grid settings.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 13, 'The shallowest mapped value on the Ekene map at a 100 m cell is 1539.72 m at grid location (1400, 2000). What is the important fact about that location?', '["It sits on Ekene-3, so the crest is a measurement","It sits outside the extrapolation limit, so the value is unsupported","It sits 300 m from the nearest well, so no measurement was ever taken there","It sits at the centre of the grid frame, so it is the best supported node on the map"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene-3 is at (1400, 2300), so the crest node lies 300 m away from it and the crest is not at a well. The node is live and legitimately mapped, and the value is still an artefact of the interpolator rather than an observation.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 14, 'The 1540 m contour on the Ekene map is a single polyline that closes on itself. Why does that closure exist?', '["Because Ekene-3 and Ekene-6 both picked shallower than 1540 m","Because the spline bows 1.28 m above the shallowest pick in the well data","Because the contour interval was set to 10 m rather than 5 m","Because the convex hull is a closed polygon, so the shallowest contour inherits its shape"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The shallowest pick is 1541 m, so without the overshoot there would be no mapped ground above 1540 m at all, and ground shallower than 1540 m exists on the map and nowhere in the pick list. The loop also excludes Ekene-3 itself, so the map displays a closed structural high containing no measurement, which is exactly the geometry a prospect gets proposed on.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-extrapolation-and-honesty', 15, 'How should the 1539.72 m value be reported?', '["As the shallowest mapped node, 1.28 m above the shallowest pick","As the crest of the structure, since it is the shallowest value the map contains","As a measurement, since it came from an exact interpolator that honours all six picks","As unusable, since any value that overshoots the pick list should be removed from the map"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The discipline is to check whether the mapped crest coincides with a control point, compare it against the shallowest pick, and state the overshoot explicitly, so the report also records that the node sits 300 m from the nearest well. Calling it the crest of the structure hands a reader an interpolator property as if it were a discovery, and deleting it would throw away the best available estimate at that node.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 1, 'What is a contour on the Ekene depth map?', '["A line joining points where the mapped surface has the same depth","A line joining the well locations in order of increasing depth","The boundary of the area that survived the 800 m extrapolation limit","A line showing where the surface dips most steeply"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A contour is a line of constant depth traced through the grid, so the 1550 m contour runs along everywhere the surface reads exactly 1550 m. The mask boundary and the steepest dip are different features that a contour set can reveal but does not define.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 2, 'The contouring code reaches a cell where one of the four corner nodes is dead. What does it do?', '["It uses the three live corners and interpolates the fourth","It skips the cell entirely, so the contour stops at the edge of the masked area","It draws the contour across the cell and marks it with a dashed line","It fills the dead corner with the mean of the live nodes and carries on"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cell with any dead corner is skipped, so lines stop where the control stops rather than being drawn on invented ground. Filling the missing corner by any rule would put a drawn line where the map has no data, which is what the mask exists to prevent.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 3, 'A contour polyline on the Ekene map ends without returning to its starting point. What does that tell you?', '["The contour value was chosen badly and lies outside the mapped range","The engine failed to chain the segments because the endpoints did not match","The line ran into either the mask or the edge of the grid frame","The surface is flat along that line, so the contour has nowhere to go"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Segments share bitwise identical endpoints along every cell edge, so chaining does not fail; a polyline stops only when it runs out of cells to continue into, at the mask boundary or at the frame edge. A polyline that comes back to its own start is a closed contour.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 4, 'On a depth map, what do closely spaced contours indicate about the surface?', '["That the rock is deep there, since crowded lines mark the deepest part of the map","That the surface is dipping steeply, since a step is crossed in a short distance","That the well control is dense there, since more picks allow more lines to be drawn","That the contour interval was set too fine in that part of the map"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each line sits a fixed depth from the next, so a short horizontal gap between lines means the surface loses that depth quickly. Reading crowded lines as deep rather than steep is the classic beginner error, and the interval is one choice for the whole map rather than something that varies across it.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 5, 'A closed contour labelled 1540 m on the Ekene depth map encloses ground whose values are smaller than 1540. What is inside the loop?', '["A structural low, since the enclosed values are below the contour value","An area of missing data, since values below the shallowest contour are not mapped","A structural high, since smaller depth means shallower rock","Nothing that can be judged without knowing the contour interval"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Depth is measured positive downwards, so the small numbers are the shallow ground and a loop drawn round them is a high. Reading it as a low is the hiking map habit, where the numbers are elevations and the big numbers are the summits.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 6, 'Of the 201 live nodes on the Ekene map, only 14 lie within 100 m of a well, yet the printed surface is smooth everywhere. What does that smoothness tell you?', '["Nothing about how well the surface is known","That the six picks agree well with each other, since disagreement would show as roughness","That the interpolation succeeded, so the mapped values can be treated as measurements","That the cell size was chosen well, since a poor choice would show as steps in the surface"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Smoothness is a property of the method rather than of the data. The method minimises bending, so it is just as smooth over ground with no control as over ground with a well nearby. The line drawn through hard data and the line drawn through pure inference are the same weight of ink, which is why smoothness must never be read as accuracy.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 7, 'The Ekene map at a 100 m cell runs from 1539.72 m to 1590 m and is contoured at 10 m, giving six lines rather than the ten asked for. Why six?', '["Four levels were dropped because they fell outside the masked area","The raw step of a little over 5 was rounded up, giving a 10 m interval","Six wells were used, so the engine drew one contour for each control point","The engine reduced the count because the deepest levels had too few live nodes to cross"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A range of about 50 m divided by a target of ten gives a raw step just over 5, and rounding up sends it to 10 m, so the levels are 1540, 1550, 1560, 1570, 1580 and 1590, which is six lines. The line count follows from the interval rather than from the number of wells.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 8, 'The Ekene map deepens to 1590 m and rises to a crest of 1539.72 m. What is the difference in status between those two numbers?', '["Both are measurements, since the gridding method honours every pick","Both are interpolated, since the map is a surface rather than a set of picks","1590 m is Ekene-4''s own pick, while 1539.72 m is shallower than any pick in the field","1539.72 m is Ekene-3''s pick moved onto the nearest node, while 1590 m is interpolated between wells"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The deepest mapped value sits exactly on Ekene-4''s measured 1590 m, so the deep end of the map is anchored by a well. The crest of 1539.72 m is shallower than Ekene-3''s 1541 m, so no rock has been seen at that depth, there is no measurement behind the number, and it is a smooth surface bending above its highest pin.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 9, 'Ekene-6 picks 1546 m and Ekene-4 picks 1590 m about 990 m apart, while Ekene-6 to Ekene-2 is about 716 m for 19 m of depth. How does that asymmetry show on the contoured map?', '["The northeast carries the closest contour spacing on the map","Both flanks look alike, because one contour interval is used across the whole map","The southern flank carries the closest spacing, because more wells sit along it","The northeast is drawn with a finer contour interval so that the steeper dip is visible"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Roughly 44 m per kilometre northeast against roughly 27 m per kilometre south means the same 10 m step is crossed in about half the distance on the northeast flank, so its lines crowd while the southern flank falls away as a broad, widely spaced slope. The interval is a single choice for the whole map and never varies from one flank to another.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 10, 'You are asked whether Ekene has a closed structural high in the centre. What does the map support?', '["Yes, because the contours wrap right around the shallow area between Ekene-3 and Ekene-6","Yes, because the crest of 1539.72 m is shallower than every well in the field","No, because a high needs at least four wells around it and only two sit near the crest","The control is consistent with a high, but the map is masked at 800 m from control"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Only 201 of the 500 nodes are live, and beyond 800 m from the nearest well nothing is drawn, so there are no contours out on the flanks to close and whether the structure closes is unknown. A closure claim needs the surface mapped all the way round, which takes more wells or seismic coverage.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 11, 'A location sits inside the grid frame. Three of the four nodes around it are live and one is dead. What does the sample return?', '["A depth blended from the three live nodes, flagged as lower confidence","The value of the nearest live node","A depth, because bilinear sampling needs only three corners","Null, because the engine will not report a depth built partly from an absence"]'::jsonb, 3, 'One dead corner is enough for the sample to return null, which matches the contouring rule that a cell with any dead corner is skipped. A location just outside the mapped area gets no number rather than a slightly less reliable one.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 12, 'P-1 at (1600, 1600) samples at 1542.62 m. The nearest well, Ekene-6, is about 361 m away and picks 1546 m, and Ekene-3 picks 1541 m. What does checking the sample against those picks establish?', '["That 1542.62 m is correct, since it lies between two measured picks","That the value is consistent with the surrounding control and no digit has slipped","That the value is too shallow, since a sample should not read shallower than the nearest well","That P-1 should be reported as 1546 m, the nearest measured value"]'::jsonb, 1, 'P-1 sits between Ekene-6 at 1546 m and the shallower Ekene-3 at 1541 m, so a value between them and nearer the shallow end is what a smooth surface should give. The check does not prove the value correct, and nothing short of drilling would. A return of 1552 m or 1538 m would have failed the check and sent you looking for a units error or a coordinate swap.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 13, 'The same six picks gridded at a 200 m cell give a deepest mapped value of 1578.04 m, while Ekene-4 picks 1590 m. What has happened?', '["The coarse grid averaged Ekene-4 with its neighbours, which pulled the deep value up","The 800 m mask removed the nodes around Ekene-4 at that cell size","No node lands on Ekene-4 at a 200 m cell, so the deepest pick in the dataset never appears on the map","The deepest pick was rejected because it sits at the edge of the control"]'::jsonb, 2, 'At a 200 m cell only Ekene-1 falls exactly on a node and the other five wells sit between nodes, so the surface is never sampled at Ekene-4''s own location. The map still looks finished, but it no longer shows the deepest measurement in the field.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 14, 'The 100 m Ekene map is contoured at 10 m and the 200 m map at 5 m. What should you conclude from the coarser map carrying more contour lines?', '["Nothing about quality: the mapped range shrank, so the automatic interval got finer while the support got worse","That the 200 m map resolves more structure, since a finer interval shows more detail","That the 200 m map was drawn by a different method, since the interval rule would otherwise give the same step","That the 100 m map is under contoured and should be redrawn at 5 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The interval is chosen from the mapped range, and the coarse map''s range narrowed because its deepest mapped value fell to 1578.04 m, so the step rounded to 5 m. Taking a finer interval as evidence of a better map is the error this comparison is built to expose.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-reading-the-map', 15, 'A map is presented as being built on 794 live nodes. What does that number actually tell you?', '["That the map is better constrained than the 201 node version of the same field","That 794 depths were measured across the field","That at least 794 wells worth of information went into the map","Only how densely the same six picks were sampled at that cell size"]'::jsonb, 3, '794 live nodes at a 50 m cell, 201 at 100 m and 50 at 200 m all come from the same six picks, so a node count is a statement about sampling rather than about knowledge. Quoting a live node count without its cell size invites the reader to hear it as authority.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 1, 'What is the order of the five workflow steps used in this course?', '["Assemble control, choose the frame and cell, interpolate, mask, present and read","Choose the frame and cell, assemble control, mask, interpolate, present and read","Assemble control, interpolate, choose the frame and cell, present and read, mask","Assemble control, choose the frame and cell, mask, interpolate, present and read"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every step constrains the ones that follow, so the sequence is fixed. Masking comes after interpolation because it deletes values the interpolator has already produced, and the frame has to exist before there is anything to interpolate onto.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 2, 'A well reached the interval but the top could not be picked, because the section was faulted out. How should that well be handled in the control set?', '["Enter the depth as zero so that the well count stays complete","Record it as absent, since a zero would be honoured as a real pick","Enter the pick from the nearest well so that the map keeps its coverage","Enter a prognosis depth, since a prognosis is better than no constraint at all"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A zero is a control point claiming the surface comes to sea level, and the interpolator cannot tell that it was meant to mean nothing, so a spline will honour it as faithfully as a real pick. A prognosis or a borrowed neighbouring value is the same failure in a less obvious form, because both put a fictional constraint into the grid.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 3, 'With the frame origin at (400, 800), two cells of padding outside the control and a 100 m cell, what does the Ekene grid come out at?', '["20 columns by 25 rows, giving 500 nodes","25 columns by 20 rows, 500 nodes","25 columns by 20 rows, 201 live nodes","It depends on how many of the six wells fall exactly on nodes"]'::jsonb, 1, '25 times 20 gives the 500 node total, and every one of those nodes is a location the interpolator will be asked for a depth at. The 201 is the live count after masking, which is a different quantity from the size of the frame.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 4, 'Rebuilding the Ekene grid at a 50 m cell raises the node count from 500 to 1575. What has that gained?', '["More detail in the surface, since finer cells resolve structure the coarse grid missed","More control, since each extra node acts as a further constraint on the spline","A better contour interval, since the engine can now lay out finer levels","Nothing in information terms, since the same surface is only sampled more finely"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Six picks are six picks. The cell size sets how finely the surface produced by the method is sampled and therefore how many numbers you end up carrying around. It adds no data, which is why the cell size has to be quoted whenever a node count is quoted.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 5, 'Which workflow step removes information rather than adding it, and why is that a feature?', '["Masking, because everything it deletes was produced by the interpolator","Interpolation, because a spline smooths away detail that is present in the picks","Contouring, because only six depth levels survive out of a continuous surface","Choosing the cell size, because a coarse cell discards nodes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 800 m limit sets 299 of the 500 nodes to null and leaves 201 live, and every value it deletes was invented rather than measured. The blank corners are the honest statement that six wells about a kilometre apart do not constrain the surface out at the frame edges.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 6, 'The gridding method used here passes through every control point. What is the quickest way to confirm that on the Ekene map?', '["Confirm that the crest of the map matches the shallowest pick of 1541 m","Confirm that the mean of the live nodes matches the mean of the six picks","Confirm that the deepest value on the map is exactly Ekene-4''s pick of 1590 m","Confirm that all 500 nodes carry a value"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The deep end of the map is anchored at 1590 m because nothing pulls the surface deeper than the deepest pick, so a mismatch there exposes coordinate, unit and frame errors at once. The shallow end does not behave the same way, since the surface overshoots to 1539.72 m.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 7, 'The Ekene crest is 1539.72 m, which is 1.28 m shallower than the shallowest pick in the field. What is the overshoot check for?', '["Removing the overshoot by clipping the surface back to the shallowest pick","Noticing the overshoot and judging whether its size is reasonable","Confirming that the shallowest pick was measured correctly","Deciding whether the contour interval needs to be made finer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The check is to notice the overshoot and to be able to explain it. About a metre on 49 m of relief is the geometry of a smooth sheet bending through six pins and is unremarkable, while an overshoot of 30 m would mean the method was inventing structure and would send you back to the interpolation step. A crest shallower than every pick is a number to explain rather than a discovery to celebrate.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 8, 'One well''s easting was entered in kilometres instead of metres. Which of the six checks catches it?', '["The mask check, since that well would fall outside the 800 m limit","The settings check, since the cell size would no longer match the node count","The sampling stability check, since P-1 would then move with cell size","The honour check, since the map would not reproduce that well''s pick where it is posted"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pick is still a genuine constraint, but it now sits somewhere the well is not, so the surface no longer honours the value at the posted location. That single test catches coordinate errors, unit errors and frame errors together.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 9, 'Why must a live node count never be quoted without the cell size beside it?', '["Because a node count is only meaningful once the contour interval is stated as well","Because the frame origin shifts with cell size, so the counts refer to different rectangles","Because the count describes sampling density rather than how well the map is constrained","Because the mask distance changes with cell size, so live counts are not comparable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The same six picks give 794 live nodes at 50 m, 201 at 100 m and 50 at 200 m. Those three numbers describe one surface at three sampling densities, and six wells is six wells at every one of them. A reader given 794 live nodes without the cell size may conclude the map is better constrained than the 201 node version, which it is not.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 10, 'P-1 reads 1542.620 m at a 50 m cell, at a 100 m cell and at a 200 m cell. What does that agreement tell you?', '["That the value is proved correct, since three independent methods agree on it","That the grid is now fine enough, so no further checks are needed","That the value is set by the control rather than node placement","That P-1 must fall exactly on a node in all three grids"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Three quite different lattices recovering the same depth says the number is driven by the interpolated surface rather than by the accident of node placement. The same method was used all three times, so this is a stability test rather than an independent confirmation.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 11, 'Which of the six graded capstone quantities carry a tolerance rather than being graded exactly?', '["The crest and the depth at P-1, both to 0.5 m","The crest and the contour interval, both to 0.5 m","The mapped node count and the crest, since both depend on the cell size","None of them, since every quantity is read straight off the panel"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The two depths are the only graded quantities with a tolerance, and 0.5 m is generous enough that careful reading off the panel always clears it. The control point count, the grid width and the mapped node count are counts, so there is no partial credit and 24 columns is as wrong as 26.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 12, 'A learner reports the capstone crest as 1541 m. What is wrong with that answer?', '["It is a rounding slip that the 0.5 m tolerance will absorb","It is Ekene-3''s measured pick rather than the shallowest depth on the map, which is 1539.72 m","It is the shallowest live node, but the question asks for the shallowest node in the whole frame","It is right at a 100 m cell but wrong at the 50 m cell the capstone uses"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The question asks for the shallowest depth on the mapped, masked surface, and the map rises about 1.28 m above its highest pin to 1539.72 m. Quoting a well pick is the wrong quantity rather than a rounding error, and it falls outside the tolerance in any case.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 13, 'What happens if you fail a module quiz three times?', '["The module reopens for reading and the quiz is rebuilt from a fresh bank","The pass mark drops to 70 percent for the remaining attempts","The capstone stays locked but the final exam becomes available anyway","A 24 hour cooldown applies before you may attempt it again"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Module quizzes are passed at 75 percent, and three failed attempts trigger a 24 hour wait. The cooldown exists so that the quizzes test understanding rather than persistence, which makes reading the lesson and working the exercise the cheapest route as well as the intended one.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 14, 'At the Professional tier the top and base surfaces are subtracted to give an isochore. What has to be true for that subtraction to mean anything?', '["The two surfaces must share exactly the same frame, and a node must be live in both","The base surface must be gridded at a finer cell size so that thickness detail is captured","The mask must be lifted from both inputs so that the isochore covers the whole frame","Both surfaces must be contoured at the same interval before they are subtracted"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Subtracting grids with different origins or cell sizes compares nodes that are not at the same place, so the frames have to match exactly. The masks interact as well, since a thickness built from one live value and one absence would be another number the map is not entitled to claim.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-mapping-workflow', 15, 'Leave one out cross validation on the Ekene control set can honestly validate only Ekene-6. Why?', '["Because Ekene-6 is the only well that carries both a top and a base pick","Because Ekene-6 lies closest to the prospect, so its residual is the most relevant one","Because it is the only well inside the hull of the other five, so its location is still mapped","Because the other five wells were used to set the frame and cannot be removed from it"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Drop a well from the outside of the pattern and its location now falls beyond what the remaining five constrain, so the masked surface has no value there to compare against. Reporting six residuals of which five were quietly extrapolated is the dishonest alternative.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'The Ekene capstone map is built from six wells, and its printed surface carries 201 live values out of a 500 node frame. How many of those printed values could coincide with a measurement, and how many wells land on a node?', '["At most 201 could coincide, since every live node is supported by data","At most six could coincide, and five of the six wells land on a node at this frame","At most six could coincide, and all six wells land on a node at this frame","At most 500 could coincide, since the frame encloses every well"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Six wells cap how many map values could possibly match a measurement, and at the capstone frame Ekene-2 misses the lattice, so only five wells sit on nodes. Even those five hold interpolated output rather than a copy of the pick.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Predict the Ekene frame at a 250 m cell size, using the control spans of 2000 m in x and 1500 m in y.', '["8 by 6, giving 48 nodes","9 by 7, giving 63 nodes","13 by 11, giving 143 nodes","12 by 10, giving 120 nodes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In x, 2000 divided by 250 is 8, plus 1 for the fence-post, plus 4 for the two-cell pad on each side gives 13; in y, 6 plus 1 plus 4 gives 11. Answering 9 by 7 means you forgot the pad, and 8 by 6 means you dropped the fence-post as well.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'A colleague reports 794 live nodes on the Ekene map and calls the field well constrained. What is wrong with the claim?', '["794 is the live count at a 50 m cell, and the same field at 200 m reports 50","794 is wrong because the capstone frame holds only 500 nodes in total","Nothing is wrong, since a higher live count means more of the area was supported by data","The claim is unsafe only because the crest moves by 0.027 m between the two settings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both counts came from the same six wells, so the count says nothing about how well the field is understood. Live node counts look like results but are properties of the grid, and changing the cell size moves them by more than a factor of fifteen with no new data. This is why a node count should never be quoted without the cell size beside it.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Which pairing of a decision to the number it sets is correct?', '["The cell size sets the live node count, and the extrapolation limit sets the total node count","The interpolation sets the total node count, and the cell size sets the crest","The contour interval sets the total node count, and the interpolation decides liveness","The cell size and the pad set the total node count, and the 800 m limit decides liveness"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The frame decision fixes how many nodes exist, and the extrapolation decision fixes how many of them are allowed to go live. Both influence the live count, which is why the two must be kept apart when reporting: one is coverage and resolution, the other is honesty.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'A well with no pick for the mapped surface, and a null node on the grid, are both records of absence. What do they have in common in this course?', '["Both are filled with the mean of the surrounding values before the map is drawn","Both are errors that must be resolved before gridding can proceed","Neither is a zero nor a number waiting to be filled in","Both are counted in the live node total so that the mapped area stays constant"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A missing pick is one fewer control point and a null node is a deliberate blank, and each states that the data does not reach there, so absent data expressed as absent data is information. Expressing either as an invented number produces an error no downstream user can see.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Why does the engine pad the control box by two cells instead of gridding exactly to the outermost wells?', '["To make the total node count a round number","To leave room for the extrapolation limit to be applied","To keep the frame square in both directions","Because contours could not close around an edge well"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The pad gives the surface somewhere to go beyond the edge wells so that contours can close. It is not what creates the extrapolation limit, which is a separate rule applied later and measured in metres from the nearest control point.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Suppose you built the Ekene frame as 24 by 19 rather than 25 by 20. What would go wrong, and how would you find out?', '["The map would refuse to draw, so the mistake would be caught at once","The frame would hold 456 nodes, and the map would still draw normally","The crest depth would move by tens of metres, which would be obvious against the picks","The six control points would no longer be honoured by the interpolation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Dropping the fence-post shifts every downstream number and quietly covers less ground than you think, because 24 times 19 is 456 rather than 500 and the last node falls 100 m short of where the pad intended. Nothing complains, which is exactly why the rule has to be applied deliberately every time a distance becomes a node count.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Ekene-1 is reported at 1548 m and P-1 at 1542.62 m. What is the difference in kind between those two statements?', '["1548 m is a measurement made in a borehole","1548 m is a model value while 1542.62 m is a measurement taken at the prospect","Both are measurements, since P-1 sits between four nodes that each hold data","Both are model values, since the interpolation recomputes every well pick"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A pick is a statement about rock, while 1542.62 m is a value the model computed at a location no well has reached, and the two carry very different weight when someone is deciding whether to spend money. The pick does not change when you change gridding settings; the node value can.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'Why is the mapped estimate at P-1 dominated by Ekene-6 rather than by the other wells?', '["Ekene-6 has the shallowest pick in the field","Ekene-6 is about 361 m from P-1 and the next nearest is about 728 m","Ekene-6 lies on the correlation section line, so it was picked most carefully","Ekene-6 is the only well that lands exactly on a grid node"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The offsets from P-1 to Ekene-6 are 300 m in x and 200 m in y, giving about 361 m, and Ekene-3 is about 728 m away with the rest further still. Ekene-3 at 1541 m is the shallowest pick, and five of the six wells land on nodes, so neither of those distinguishes Ekene-6.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'The three cell sizes give crests of 1539.691 m at 50 m and 1539.718 m at both 100 m and 200 m. Which sentence about this map''s crest is defensible?', '["The crest is uncertain to about 0.03 m, which is the accuracy of the map","The crest depends strongly on the cell size, so the finest available grid should always be used","The crest of the modelled surface is about 1539.7 m and is stable across sampling","The crest is 1541 m, because a map cannot be shallower than its shallowest pick"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The crest is an inference from six control points rather than a measurement. Stability across cell sizes shows only that the sampling is adequate; it says nothing about whether six wells constrain the true high. Reading the 0.027 m spread as the accuracy of the map confuses a sampling difference with the uncertainty that matters.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'The 200 m map is drawn at a 5 m contour interval while the better supported 100 m map uses 10 m. What is the trap here?', '["The coarser and less supported map is the one that reads as more detailed","The finer interval means the coarse map has resolved more geology","The interval is an interpreter choice, so the two maps disagree about the structure","A 5 m interval is invalid on a surface with only 49 m of relief across the wells"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nobody asked for the finer interval; it fell out of a cell size decision made several steps earlier, because the narrower range of mapped values pushed the level chooser down, so the coarse map carries twice as many contour lines. Line density on a sheet is a presentation artefact and is not evidence of resolution.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Which statement about the 800 m extrapolation limit and the grid frame is correct?', '["The limit is what makes the frame differ between cell sizes","The limit is measured in cells, so the supported area scales with the cell size","The limit applies only outside the padded rectangle, so padded nodes are always live","The limit is a fixed distance in metres, so the same area is cut into different numbers of nodes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is why the live fraction differs across settings: 794 of 1575 at 50 m, 201 of 500 at 100 m and 50 of 195 at 200 m, on identical control. It is the pad, not the extrapolation limit, that is measured in cells and so changes the frame with the cell size.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'You quote a mean mapped depth of 1550.27 m in a report. What has to go with it?', '["The number of contour lines drawn on the finished map sheet","The coordinates of the frame origin and its cell size","The control count of six wells, and the cell size","The maximum node value found on the mapped surface"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Six is the number that sets how much the map is entitled to claim, and quoting a mean to two decimals without it implies a precision the data does not support. Node counts need the cell size for the same reason, since they move by a factor of fifteen with no new data.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Why does a map need control spread out when a correlation section can work with wells strung along a line?', '["A map is two dimensional, so every well is posted in its true relative position","Mapping algorithms reject collinear control points outright","A section needs more wells than a map because it has no vertical exaggeration","A map can only use wells that happen to sit on the grid lattice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A correlation section is one dimensional in map view and cannot represent a well off to the side, which is why Ekene-5 and Ekene-6 were of no use there and are essential here. Wells strung along a line say almost nothing about the surface perpendicular to that line, so a map needs more control than a section and it needs that control spread out.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Triangulation extends nowhere beyond the outer triangles of the control set, while a thin-plate spline can be evaluated at any point in the frame. So why does the Ekene spline map still have an outer boundary?', '["Because the spline value falls back to the null sentinel once no well is in range","Because the hull test and the 800 m distance test mask nodes after the spline has already been fitted","Because the solver refuses to evaluate nodes outside the convex hull and raises an error","Because the grid frame was drawn to match the outer triangles of the control set"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The spline would happily supply a smooth value at every one of the 500 frame nodes. The boundary is imposed afterwards by masking, which is why 299 nodes are blank by design rather than because the arithmetic failed.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'If the six Ekene picks were mapped with inverse distance weighting instead of a spline, what would the shallowest mapped value be?', '["1539.7181 m, the same crest, because both methods honour the same six picks","1550.27 m, the mean of the mapped surface","1541 m, because a weighted average cannot leave the range of the data","1546 m, the pick at the well nearest the crest node"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An averaging method is trapped inside the range of its inputs, so nothing can come out shallower than the shallowest pick. The 1.28 m overshoot that produces the closed 1540 m contour is a property of the spline, so changing method would remove that closure without a single new measurement being made.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'A colleague points at the closed 1540 m contour on the Ekene map and proposes a well on the crest. What is the strongest objection?', '["The crest node carries a null, so no depth exists there to drill to","The contour interval of 10 m is too coarse to define a crest","The spline failed to honour Ekene-3, which is why the map runs shallower than 1541 m","The crest sits at no well, 300 m from Ekene-3, and is 1.28 m shallower than any pick in the field"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The closure exists because a smooth sheet bows above its own control between Ekene-3 and Ekene-6. Nothing went wrong with the fit and the value is still the best estimate at that node, but reading the closure as a discovered structural high treats an interpolator property as structure.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'The engine''s default extrapolation limit is two grid cells, which is 200 m at a 100 m cell, and this course overrides it to 800 m. What is the reasoning behind 800 m?', '["Ekene''s wells are roughly a kilometre apart, so 800 m is about half that","800 m is the distance at which the spline stops overshooting the pick list","800 m keeps the live count near 200, which is a convenient number to report","800 m is eight grid cells, which is the standard multiple for structural mapping"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The limit is argued from well spacing rather than from the grid: Ekene-3 to Ekene-6 is about 707 m, Ekene-2 to Ekene-6 about 716 m and Ekene-1 to Ekene-5 about 985 m. Extending about half a well spacing past the data is defensible in a review, and anything further should be a deliberate written claim.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Three wells lie on a straight line and are the only control available. What does the engine do?', '["It fits a plane through the three points and maps the whole frame","It refuses the job, because the system is singular","It decimates the control set and fits whatever points remain","It maps only the nodes lying within 800 m of the line"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The solver needs at least three control points and rejects a system that is singular, which is what three collinear points give, since they define no unique surface. Raising an error is better than returning a surface the method cannot justify.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'A mapped depth of 1542.6199 m carries far more digits than a well pick written as 1546 m. What should you conclude?', '["The mapped value is the more accurate of the two, because it resolves the surface more finely","The pick should be rewritten with matching precision so that the two can be compared","The digits come from float arithmetic rather than from knowledge","The mapped value must be a measurement, because only measurements are reported to four decimal places"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Precision is part of the trap, because extra decimal places make an interpolated value look more authoritative than the measurements it was derived from, and the mapped value is the less certain of the two. The pick is the hard fact and the mapped node is inference, whichever of them is printed with more digits.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'The mapped depth at P-1 is 1542.62 m and the nearest control is Ekene-6, about 361 m away, which picked 1546 m. Which pair of statements should a mapper hold at the same time?', '["It is the best available estimate for a prognosis at P-1, and the map cannot prove it","It is unusable for a prognosis, and the nearest pick of 1546 m should be quoted in its place","It is a measurement, because P-1 lies well inside the supported part of the grid","It is unreliable because it disagrees with Ekene-6, so the map should be regridded until they agree"]'::jsonb, 0, 'No measurement at P-1 exists, so the map cannot prove the number. Refusing the first statement makes you useless, because decisions still have to be made and there is no better number on offer. Forgetting the second makes you dangerous, because a prognosis stated with no uncertainty gets planned against as though it were certain.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Which use of an interpolated node value is illegitimate?', '["Putting it on a well prognosis with the nearest control distance stated alongside it","Subtracting it from a base surface at the same node to give an isochore thickness","Summing it with the other live nodes to give a gross rock volume","Quoting it as evidence that the surface behaves smoothly between the wells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The map was built from a smoothness assumption, so reading smooth behaviour back off it proves nothing and is circular. Interpolated values do real work in prediction, differencing and volumes, and none of that work makes them evidence.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'A learner reports the mean depth of the Ekene map as about 6 times 10 to the 29. What did they do?', '["They averaged the six picks instead of the grid nodes","They averaged all 500 frame nodes with the nulls included","They summed the live nodes without dividing by the live count","They used the 200 m grid, where far fewer nodes are live"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The sentinel is 1.0e30, so 299 of them swamp any sum of real depths. Statistics from a gridded surface run over live nodes only, which is the same rule as excluding nulls from a log average, and the correct answer here is 1550.27 m over 201 live nodes.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'You are told that a surface has 794 live nodes. What must accompany that number before it means anything?', '["The number of wells whose picks went into the fit","The contour interval used to draw the map","The cell size, because the same map has 201 live nodes at 100 m","The depth range covered by the mapped surface"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A live count is a mapped area divided by whatever cell size you happened to pick, so on its own it fixes nothing. The same map holds 201 live nodes at a 100 m cell and 50 at 200 m. Converting to area gives a number that survives a change of settings: 794 nodes at a 50 m cell is 1,985,000 square metres, close to the 2 square kilometres the other two grids report.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Ekene-4''s pick is corrected and the map is regridded at the same cell size and the same 800 m limit. What changes?', '["The live count changes, because the mask follows the shape of the new surface","Every live node takes a new depth, while the same 201 nodes stay live","Only the nodes near Ekene-4 change, and the live count changes with them","Nothing changes until the contour interval is recomputed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fit is global, so resolving the system moves every node on the map. The mask depends on well positions, the convex hull and the distance limit, none of which changed, so which nodes are live is untouched while what they hold is not.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Account for all 500 nodes of the Ekene frame at a 100 m cell.', '["299 blank by design, at most six on a well, and roughly 195 from the spline","299 blank because the solver failed there, and 201 measured at or close to wells","All 500 live, of which 299 are extrapolated and 201 interpolated","201 blank by design and 299 live, of which six are measurements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The 299 nulls are the map declining to answer where the hull or the distance test is not satisfied, which is output rather than failure. Of the 201 live nodes at most six coincide with a well, so about 97 percent of the mapped values came from the interpolator.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'What is the check that separates a mapped extreme which is a measurement from one which is an invention?', '["Check whether the extreme lies inside the convex hull","Check whether the extreme survives a change of cell size","Compare the mapped minimum and maximum against the pick list","Check whether the extreme is quoted to four decimal places"]'::jsonb, 2, 'On Ekene the mapped maximum of 1590 m equals Ekene-4''s pick, so the map is reporting data there, while the mapped minimum of 1539.7181 m matches no pick and is reporting the algorithm. That asymmetry is a fact about this arrangement of six wells, and the comparison is the only way you find it.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'Two maps are made from the same six picks and one of them is visibly smoother. What does the extra smoothness tell you about accuracy?', '["The smoother map is more accurate, because it carries fewer artefacts","The smoother map is less accurate, because smoothing always removes real structure","The smoother map is more accurate wherever it stays inside the range of the picks","Nothing, because smoothness is a property of the method rather than the data"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every method returns the picks at the picks, and both maps honour the same six picks, so they differ only in the space between, where no measurement can referee the argument. Smoothness is what makes a spline map look credible, and it looks equally credible where there is no data at all.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'The Ekene surface runs from 1539.72 m to 1590 m and the mean of the live nodes is 1550.27 m. What does a mean that low in the range say about the map?', '["Most of the mapped area lies towards the shallow end","Half the mapped area is shallower than 1550.27 m and half is deeper","The map is dominated by the deep northeast, which drags the mean below the midpoint of the range","The mean has no meaning on a masked surface, since 299 nodes were removed before it was computed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The midpoint of the range is about 1565 m, so a mean of 1550.27 m says most live nodes carry shallow values and the deep ground around Ekene-4 covers only a small area. The mean of the nodes is an area weighted average rather than a median, so it does not split the map in half.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'No well on Ekene picks shallower than 1541 m, yet the map carries a 1540 m contour. How can that line exist?', '["The contour levels are laid out from the well picks, so 1540 is the rounded value of the 1541 m pick","The mapped crest is 1539.72 m, because the spline bends slightly above its highest control point","The 1540 m contour is drawn through the dead nodes, where the surface is unconstrained","It is an artefact of converting contour polylines from grid indices into map coordinates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Levels are laid out from the mapped range rather than from the picks, and the mapped range starts at 1539.72 m because a smooth sheet through six pins can rise slightly above the highest of them, so ground shallower than 1540 m exists on the map. The 1540 m line therefore runs across ground where no rock has been measured.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'You sample the 100 m Ekene grid at Ekene-6''s own location, (1900, 1800). What comes back, and why?', '["A blend of the four surrounding nodes, which will be close to 1546 m without equalling it","1542.62 m, since P-1 and Ekene-6 fall inside the same cell","Null, because a sample cannot be taken at a control point","1546 m, because the location lands exactly on a node"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With the origin at (400, 800) and a 100 m cell, that location is exactly 15 cells across and 10 cells up, so the fractional part is zero in both directions, no neighbour carries any weight, and only that one node needs to be live. The surface passes through every pick, so the node carries Ekene-6''s measured 1546 m.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'A sample is requested at (400, 2700), the far northwest corner of the grid frame. What does the engine return?', '["The nearest live value, since the point is inside the frame","Nothing, because a 25 by 20 frame does not reach that corner","Null, because the corner is more than 800 m from any well","A depth extrapolated from the spline, flagged as beyond the control"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The point is inside the frame but outside the mapped area, so the nodes around it are dead and the sample refuses to blend dead nodes. A location beyond the extrapolation limit gets no number at all rather than a less reliable one.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'You run the six quality control checks on the 200 m version of the Ekene map. Which one fails, and how?', '["The mask check, because only 50 of the 195 nodes are live","The overshoot check, because the crest still reads 1539.72 m","The settings check, because the contour interval fell to 5 m","The honour check, because the deepest mapped value is 1578.04 m"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Honouring the control means the map reproduces each pick at that pick''s own location, and at a 200 m cell no node lands on Ekene-4, so the deepest measurement in the field is absent from the surface while Ekene-4 picks 1590 m. A live count of 50 and a crest of 1539.72 m are both expected behaviour rather than failures.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Why does the Ekene map stop exactly at 1590 m at the deep end while rising past every pick at the shallow end?', '["Nothing in the control pulls the surface deeper than the deepest pick in the field","The mask is applied from the deep end upward, so it clips deep values but leaves shallow ones alone","The engine clamps the surface at the deepest pick and leaves the shallow end free","Ekene-4 carries more weight in the fit than Ekene-3, because it sits at the edge of the control"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The surface passes through all six picks and is free to run outside the range of the data, and on this geometry it does so only over the high. The 1.28 m overshoot at the crest is the behaviour of the method rather than a clamp or a masking rule.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'What must be true before the capstone unlocks?', '["Every lesson read and all six module quizzes passed at 75 percent","Every lesson read, all six module quizzes at 75 percent, and the final exam at 70 percent","The final exam passed at 75 percent, with the module quizzes optional","Every lesson read and the final exam passed at 70 percent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gates run in a fixed sequence checked server side: lessons, then each module quiz at 75 percent, then the final exam at 70 percent across the whole course, then the capstone. Passing the capstone is what grants the Associate certification.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'A colleague shows you a map of the same six Ekene picks with 50 live nodes and a 5 m contour interval, and says it resolves more structure than the 201 node map. What is the right response?', '["Agree, since a finer interval draws more lines across the same relief","Agree, since a smaller live count means a tighter mask and so a more honest map","Disagree: 50 live nodes means a 200 m cell, and the mapped range narrowed","Ask for the frame origin, since node counts cannot be compared"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two classic errors are combined here, quoting a node count without its cell size and reading a finer interval as a better map. The coarse grid drops to a deepest mapped value of 1578.04 m, so its range shrank and the automatic step rounded to 5 m while its support got worse.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Someone wants the Ekene high written up as a closure. What is the defensible answer?', '["It can be called a closure once the crest of 1539.72 m has been confirmed by a well","It can be called a closure, since the mapped area contains a clear shallow core","It cannot, so the map should be regridded with a larger extrapolation limit until the contours close","It cannot, because the map stops 800 m from control and the shape beyond that is an assumption"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A closure is a claim that the surface turns over and comes back up on every side, which needs the surface mapped on every side, so closing it needs more wells or seismic across the flanks. Loosening the extrapolation limit until the lines close would manufacture the answer by drawing invented ground in the same ink as the well control.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Which of the six graded capstone quantities is unchanged if the grid is rebuilt at a different cell size?', '["The mapped node count","The control point count","The crest","The contour interval"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The control point count of 6 comes from step 1 of the workflow, before any gridding happens, so no setting can move it. The live node count, the crest and the interval all shift with the settings, which is why the capstone fixes the cell at 100 m.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'A prospect depth you are about to put in a well proposal comes back different at each of three cell sizes, while P-1 reads 1542.620 m at all three. What does the contrast between the two locations mean?', '["The unstable location is being carried by the interpolator rather than by the data","The unstable location needs a finer cell size, after which its value can be quoted normally","The two cannot be compared, since stability depends on the contour interval","There is a bad pick near the unstable location and it should be dropped from the control set"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A value that swings with cell size is the grid resolution talking, which happens far from control, near the edge of the mask, or where the interpolator is bridging a large empty gap, so it should be quoted with that caveat or not quoted at all. P-1 sits about 361 m from Ekene-6 and reads the same depth on three quite different lattices, so its value is set by the control.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Suppose the Ekene map had come back with a crest 30 m shallower than the shallowest pick instead of 1.28 m. What would that call for?', '["Nothing, since an overshoot of any size is normal behaviour for a smooth surface","Reporting the crest as the shallowest pick of 1541 m instead","Going back to the interpolation step, since the method would be inventing structure","Tightening the extrapolation limit below 800 m until the crest falls back inside the range of the picks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'About a metre on 49 m of relief is the geometry of a smooth sheet through six pins, while 30 m of unmeasured relief is the method producing structure that no well supports. The check exists to size the overshoot and act on it, not to hide it by quoting a pick or by tightening the mask.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'A prospect map drawn at a 5 m interval is presented beside the regional map it came from, which is drawn at 25 m, and the prospect flank looks far steeper. What should you check first?', '["The well count on each map, since the prospect map should carry more control","Which map is the more recent, since the newer one supersedes the older","Both legends, since the flank may have five times as many lines across it at the same dip","The crest value on each map, since a steeper flank should come with a shallower crest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Comparing maps drawn at different intervals is a standard way to be misled and it is easy to do by accident. Until the interval and the cell size match on both, the maps are not comparable and one of them has to be redrawn.'),
  ('mapping', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Where does the beginner mapping workflow sit in the wider chain, and what does the 800 m mask hand on?', '["Mapping starts the chain, since the picks are produced from the grid itself","Volumetrics feeds mapping, since a contact has to be chosen before the surface can be gridded","The chain ends at the map, since volumetrics works from well data directly rather than from surfaces","Correlation feeds the picks in, volumetrics consumes the surface, and the blanks pass on"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Correlation produces the picks, mapping turns them into a surface, and volumetrics turns the surface into a number a decision can rest on. The honest blanks propagate rather than disappearing, so an area the map refused to claim is an area the volume does not claim either.');
